// Nodes that keep taking parents that come later in the tree's order
// (Inputs/tree_churn.ent): each goes to the order's end with what is
// below it, the order fills with the gaps they leave, and is made again
// when it has no room. The sums are those of the same tree sorted, which
// is put in order from scratch for every change, and whose table, with
// room for 4 edges and 4 in it, takes each new parent in the old one's
// place.
// RUN: %ent run %S/Inputs/tree_churn.ent 2>/dev/null > %t.out
// RUN: sed 's/ tree capacity 4/ tree sorted capacity 4/' \
// RUN:     %S/Inputs/tree_churn.ent > %t.sorted.ent
// RUN: grep -q "tree sorted" %t.sorted.ent
// RUN: %ent run %t.sorted.ent 2>/dev/null | sort > %t.sorted.out
// RUN: sort %t.out | cmp - %t.sorted.out
// RUN: FileCheck %s --input-file=%t.out

// a under d, which is after it.
// CHECK:      1: root 1
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: a 10011
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: b 101
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: c 1001
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: d 10001
// d under c, under b, under a, under the root: the order is full.
// CHECK:      5: root 1
// CHECK-NEXT: 5: a 11
// CHECK-NEXT: 5: b 111
// CHECK-NEXT: 5: c 1111
// CHECK-NEXT: 5: d 11111
// b under d: no room for it at the end, so the order is made again.
// CHECK:      7: root 1
// CHECK-NEXT: 7: a 11
// CHECK-NEXT: 7: b 11101
// CHECK-NEXT: 7: c 1001
// CHECK-NEXT: 7: d 11001
// CHECK-NEXT: 8: root 1
// CHECK-NEXT: 8: a 11111
// CHECK-NEXT: 8: b 11101
// CHECK-NEXT: 8: c 1001
// CHECK-NEXT: 8: d 11001
// CHECK-NOT:  {{.}}
